Optimization and Filtering Mechanisms                                                                                

Add:                                                                                                                 

Recent advancements in multi-hop retrieval have highlighted that failures are not uniformly distributed but cluster  
in structurally predictable subpopulations. To address this, RegimeAbstain computes a Retrieval Confidence Score     
(RCS) based on query-ANN structural features, implementing a calibrated abstention policy that significantly reduces 
confident-wrong-answer rates without requiring additional LLM calls [^2609.22056]. Similarly, managing conflicting   
retrieved memories remains a critical challenge; the Memory Decision Layer (MDL) introduces a zero-parameter         
controller that fuses relevance, reliability, and task risk through orthogonal subspace projection, explicitly       
decoupling confidence from consistency to reduce hallucination rates under conflicting memories by over 56%          
[^2609.22043]. Furthermore, bridging the semantic gap between retrievers and LLMs is crucial for relationship        
modeling. ConRAG employs a lightweight Con-Former model placed between the retriever and LLM to capture and transmit 
semantic information, enabling effective feature modeling and alignment even in low-resource scenarios where both    
components are frozen [^10.1145_3847108]. Finally, hierarchical retrieval strategies have proven effective for       
grounding synthetic outputs. AURORA-RAG integrates documentary evidence across country, community, and persona stages
using Reciprocal Rank Fusion and Pseudo-Relevance Feedback, increasing corpus-supported claims to 91% and reducing   
hallucination frequency to 7% [^10.20944_preprints202609.1600.v1].                                                   

