generation alongside provenance cues to reduce hallucination risks while prioritizing process quality over direct    
answer generation [^10.3390_asi9010017]. In high-performance computing environments, AskALCF addresses complex user  
support challenges by indexing public documentation into a vector store and exposing live cluster state via Model    
Context Protocol (MCP) servers, enabling agents to combine documentation with operational context in a single tool   
call [^10.5281_zenodo.22804496]. In scientific collaborations, Data Sherpa provides context-aware access to scattered
knowledge by migrating onto Open WebUI for local model deployment and multi-agent workflows, emphasizing data        
sovereignty and the ability to explicitly acknowledge uncertainty [^10.5281_zenodo.22804319]. Additionally,          
journalistic workflows have explored cooperative production models where modular storytelling and RAG architectures  
preserve editorial control while enabling AI-assisted contextual generation and personalized news consumption        
[^10.5281_zenodo.20258770].                                                                                          
                                                                                                                     
 ## Semantic Governance and Ethical Implications                                                                     
-Emerging research critically examines the architectural limitations of source-grounding in RAG systems. Empirical   
tests across multiple RAG implementations reveal that mere source access does not prevent semantic governance        
failures or hallucinations under strict semantic dominance, indicating a need for advanced governance primitives     
[^10.5281_zenodo.18039273]. Furthermore, privacy benchmarks indicate that standard RAG assistants can inadvertently  
leak sensitive user data in over 26% of interactions, underscoring the necessity for privacy-by-design structural    
safeguards in personalized AI deployments [^10.48550_arxiv.2512.24848].                                              
+Emerging research critically examines the architectural limitations of source-grounding in RAG systems. Empirical   
tests across multiple RAG implementations reveal that mere source access does not prevent semantic governance        
failures or hallucinations under strict semantic dominance, indicating a need for advanced governance primitives     
[^10.5281_zenodo.18039273]. Furthermore, privacy benchmarks indicate that standard RAG assistants can inadvertently  
leak sensitive user data in over 26% of interactions, underscoring the necessity for privacy-by-design structural    
safeguards in personalized AI deployments [^10.48550_arxiv.2512.24848]. Privacy and capability separation are also   
critical in specialized hiring platforms. The NeuroMatch Agentic RAG architecture introduces Identity and Capability 
Separation (ICS) to decouple personally identifiable information from capability data before semantic indexing,      
combining deterministic constraints with human approval gates to ensure fairness and privacy in decision-support     
systems [^10.6084_m9.figshare.33941032].                                                                             
                                                                                                                     
 ## Advanced RAG Architectures                                                                                       
-Beyond standard QA, RAG principles are being adapted for complex agentic workflows. The R-Debater framework         
introduces argumentative memory and retrieval grounding to generate coherent, stance-aligned multi-turn debates,     
demonstrating that combining retrieval with structured planning yields more faithful and logically consistent outputs
[^https:__openalex.org_W7118051612].                                                                                 
+Beyond standard QA, RAG principles are being adapted for complex agentic workflows. The R-Debater framework         
introduces argumentative memory and retrieval grounding to generate coherent, stance-aligned multi-turn debates,     
demonstrating that combining retrieval with structured planning yields more faithful and logically consistent outputs
[^https:__openalex.org_W7118051612]. Structured multimodal frameworks are also emerging to enforce strict knowledge  
boundaries. WILDFLOW introduces an architectural control layer that prioritizes structured Markdown files as the     
authoritative source of truth, enforces unit-level vector database isolation, and restricts LLM usage to             
classification and verification, transforming retrieval into a verification-driven pipeline optimized for educational
environments [^10.5281_zenodo.18911616].                                                                             
                                                                                                                     

